Feature: Hosted services on the gateway host
  LangWatch serves the hosted services itself, so it meters them itself. A
  connected install is never asked to report its own hosted usage.

  The gateway authenticates the caller, checks that the license is entitled to
  the service and that the organization's budget has room, and only then hands
  the request to the one classifier LangWatch Cloud uses. Spend is recorded at
  the list rate under the customer organization.

  As the operator of a connected self-hosted install
  I want hosted services to be metered and capped on the LangWatch side
  So that I can see what I use and can never be invoiced past the cap I agreed

  Background:
    Given LangWatch Cloud with the gateway running
    And customer organization "ACME" with an active registered license
    And "ACME" has a prepaid commit of 1000 USD and a budget equal to it

  # ============================================================================
  # POST /v1/instant-evals/classify
  # ============================================================================

  @integration @unimplemented
  Scenario: A classify call is judged and metered under the customer organization
    Given the license is entitled to "instant_evals"
    When the install sends a text and its questions to the classify route
    Then it receives the verdicts and the input tokens that were judged
    And a spend row of request type "instant_eval" is recorded under "ACME" at the list rate
    And the row names the managed key of that license

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: The customer is charged the list rate and never sees the provider cost
    When a classify call judging 1,000,000 input tokens is recorded
    Then the recorded cost is the list rate for 1,000,000 input tokens
    And the response carries no provider cost

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: A license without the entitlement is refused
    Given the license is not entitled to "instant_evals"
    When the install calls the classify route
    Then the call is refused with code "connect_service_not_entitled"
    And nothing is judged and nothing is recorded

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: A connected customer is governed by its contract budget, not the free allowance
    Given "ACME" has no LangWatch Cloud subscription
    And "ACME" has spent more than the free Instant Evals allowance
    When the install calls the classify route within its budget
    Then the call is judged

  @integration @unimplemented
  Scenario: The budget is a hard stop
    Given "ACME" has spent its whole budget
    When the install calls the classify route
    Then the call is refused with status 402 and code "budget_exceeded"
    And nothing is judged and nothing is recorded

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: A request that is too large is refused before it is judged
    When the install sends a text larger than the classifier accepts
    Then the call is refused as too large
    And nothing is recorded

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: A judge that skips reports the reason and costs nothing
    Given the judge is rate limited upstream
    When the install calls the classify route
    Then the response reports the skip reason
    And no spend is recorded for the skipped request

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: Judged text is not stored
    When a classify call completes
    Then the judged text and the questions are not written to any LangWatch store
    And the spend row holds token counts and cost only

  # ============================================================================
  # GET /v1/usage
  # ============================================================================

  @integration @unimplemented
  Scenario: A connected install reads its usage
    Given "ACME" has spent 120 USD of its 1000 USD budget this term
    When the install reads the usage route
    Then it sees 120 USD spent, a cap of 1000 USD and 880 USD of credit remaining
    And it sees the period the figures cover
    And it sees "instant_evals" among its entitled services

  @integration @unimplemented
  Scenario: A virtual key reads its usage too
    Given a Cloud project with a virtual key and a budget
    When a call with that virtual key reads the usage route
    Then it sees the spend and the cap of the budgets that apply to that key
    And it sees no entitled services, because a virtual key is not a license

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: Usage says so when live spend is not available
    Given the spend ledger cannot be read
    When the install reads the usage route
    Then the response marks spend as unavailable
    And it does not report zero spent

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: Usage without any budget reports no cap
    Given an organization with no budget
    When the usage route is read
    Then the cap and the remaining credit are reported as not set

  # ============================================================================
  # PUT /v1/budget
  # ============================================================================

  @integration @unimplemented
  Scenario: A customer lowers its own cap
    When the install sets the cap to 400 USD
    Then the budget of "ACME" is 400 USD
    And the usage route reports the new cap

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: A cap above the prepaid commit is refused when overage is off
    Given on-demand overage is not enabled for "ACME"
    When the install sets the cap to 1500 USD
    Then the request is refused with code "connect_budget_above_contract_maximum"
    And the refusal states the maximum of 1000 USD

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: A cap may reach the commit plus the agreed overage maximum
    Given on-demand overage is enabled for "ACME" up to 500 USD
    When the install sets the cap to 1500 USD
    Then the budget of "ACME" is 1500 USD

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: A cap above the commit plus the overage maximum is refused
    Given on-demand overage is enabled for "ACME" up to 500 USD
    When the install sets the cap to 1501 USD
    Then the request is refused with code "connect_budget_above_contract_maximum"

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: A cap below what is already spent stops further use
    Given "ACME" has spent 120 USD
    When the install sets the cap to 100 USD
    Then the budget of "ACME" is 100 USD
    And the next classify call is refused with status 402

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: A virtual key cannot change a budget through this route
    When a call with a virtual key sets the cap
    Then the request is refused because only a license may change its contract cap here

  @unit @unimplemented
  Scenario: A cap that is not a positive amount is refused
    When the install sets the cap to a negative amount
    Then the request is refused as invalid
